  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 226 AM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nCurrent surface observations across the plains show low dewpoint \ndepressions due to outflow boundaries advecting low-level moisture \ninto the area. This could create areas of patchy fog as the sun \nrises this morning, but expect it to quickly burn off with daytime \nheating. Most model guidance has now backed off on a low stratus \ndeck this morning. However, with a cluster of storms moving through \nthe eastern plains early currently, will be keeping an eye on the \nformation of that stratus deck for the next few hours. If these low \nclouds do form, it will limit the severe weather threat later this \nafternoon. \n\nAs of now, we are still on track for a round of thunderstorms this \nafternoon across the forecast area, with the potential for some to \nbecome severe across the I-25 Corridor and adjacent plains. A weak \nshortwave will track across eastern Colorado this afternoon, which \nshould provide enough forcing to support scattered to numerous \nstorms. However, CAMs show a variety of solutions of storm \ndevelopment over the I-25 Corridor, ranging from isolated-to-\nscattered convection to numerous thunderstorms. This is likely \nattributed to models backing off on instability immediately off the \nfoothills, with MLCAPE generally below 1000 J/kg (aside from the \nNAM, which still shows MLCAPE up to 1200-1400 J/kg for the Denver \nmetro). Will need to keep an eye on how the environment evolves this \nmorning. Regardless, won't be surprised if we see a few severe \nthunderstorms over the I-25 Corridor given decent shear profiles. \nThese storms will be capable of producing hail and strong winds. \n\nModels are in decent agreement of storms clustering further \neast/southeast in the late afternoon/early evening as they tap into \nbetter instability and shear profiles. As of right now, have better \nconfidence in the severe weather threat for areas south of \nInterstate 76, where hail up to 1.5 inches and strong winds of 60-65 \nmph possible. There is also a flooding threat given PWAT values of \n125-150% of normal. However, given the expected fast storm motion of \nthese storms, thinking it will be more of an isolated threat \nassociated with the strongest convection. Expect thunderstorms \nactivity to be completely out of the forecast area by 10/11 PM \ntonight. \n\nWe will begin to warm up again on Thursday as the axis of the mid-\nlevel thermal ridge shifts slightly towards the forecast area. It \nlooks like most of the plains will get up to the high 80s, but some \nareas will likely reach the low 90s by the afternoon. Another \nround of scattered showers and thunderstorms is possible. However,\nexpect coverage to be more limited compared to Wednesday, given \nless moisture availability. A few storms could become severe \nacross the eastern plains with favorable instability and shear \nprofiles. However, warming aloft will likely suppress most\nconvection.\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to favor heat rebuilding over the \narea, with temperatures reaching back up to the mid-90s by Friday\nand Saturday. With the upper level ridge shifting east over \nColorado, moisture will be cut of. However, there should still be \nenough lingering low-level moisture to support a isolated \nafternoon storms. Then, by the end of the weekend/beginning of \nnext week, a strong upper level shortwave will track from the \nPacific northwest across the northern United States. This will \nlikely bring cooler temperatures once again, and another round of \nshowers and thunderstorms.",
